mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of writing style in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nThe tell lists shift as models change — attribute, **verify-quarterly**. The craft (rhythm, specificity,\ntone control) is stable; the perception layer is what 2026 changed.\n\n## The stakes, quantified (attribute)\n- **52% of consumers report they become less engaged the moment they suspect copy is AI-generated** (Bynder's\n  2,000-person UK/US study — which also found 56% *preferred* the AI article when it was unlabeled; the\n  penalty is for suspected AI, not AI per se); **only 13% of consumers say they completely trust AI**\n  (Klaviyo's ~8,000-consumer AI persona research).\n- **The audience can tell:** research finds annotators who frequently use ChatGPT for writing achieve\n  near-perfect detection of AI text, outperforming automatic detectors even against paraphrasing (Russell et\n  al., via the multilingual-detection literature). Assuming readers can't spot it is a losing bet.\n- **Machines penalize sameness too:** search systems' information-gain direction rewards unique perspectives;\n  predictable-pattern content is treated as noise, and same-prompt content across competitors erases the\n  advantage (attribute).\n\n## The tell taxonomy (converging across 2026 sources — apply as a cluster, never one marker)\n**Vocabulary:** delve, tapestry, leverage, testament, underscore, \"in today's fast-paced world\" — the \"delve\"\nsurge is measured: **44% of all PubMed appearances of \"delve\" since 1849 occurred in 2023–24 alone**\n(medRxiv), with delve+underscore co-occurring in 98.8% of their joint 2023–24 articles. **Structure:**\nrepetitive sentence shapes; overused connectors (furthermore/moreover); rigid intro–point–point–conclusion;\nforced rule-of-three (\"First… Second… Third…\"); \"It's not X, it's Y\" negative parallels; lists imposed on\nideas that don't need them; misplaced social proof in personal posts. **Substance:** fluff sentences that\nsound professional but add no information. **Texture:** relentlessly polite neutral tone; positivity bias;\nstrictly on-topic organization where humans digress; typographic perfection (proper em dashes, curly quotes,\ntrue ellipses) where fast human typing produces --, straight quotes, and ... (Paralect; PR Daily; oliviacal;\nthe arXiv slop-measurement and human-vs-AI literature).\n\n## The counter-nuance the top 1% holds (attribute)\n**No single marker proves AI — and tell-hunting overcorrects.** The em-dash panic (\"the ChatGPT hyphen\")\ncondemns punctuation great writers have always used (Rolling Stone documents the discourse); detectors are\nunreliable arbiters — the Stanford study (Liang et al., *Patterns* 2023) found seven GPT detectors **misflagged\nnon-native-English TOEFL essays as AI at a 61.3% average false-positive rate**, with all seven unanimously\nflagging 19.8% of them — while classifying US-student essays near-perfectly. The winning position: *\"What matters isn't whether your content contains em dashes —\nit's whether your content is so obviously written by a human that the question doesn't come up\"* — via deep\nthinking, personal anecdotes, fresh perspectives, original research (SALT); *\"say something only you could\nsay\"* (Paralect). Write for readers, never against detectors. One tactical footnote: if a specific audience\ncurrently reads a marker as AI, using it sparingly is a perception choice — not a truth about writing.\n\n## What human writing actually looks like (the research profile)\nVersus AI's analytical, formal, organized, relentlessly on-topic, positivity-biased profile, human text is\n**more subjective, colloquial, emotion-rich, and divergent** — it wanders, takes positions, varies rhythm\n(\"burstiness\"), and carries specific references and small imperfections (the arXiv human-vs-AI literature).\nThat profile is a craft target, honestly reached: real opinions, real specifics, varied sentence lengths —\nnever staged typos or invented anecdotes.\n\n## House metrics\nRead-through/dwell signals, saves/shares, and reply quality on published posts (native, via\nanalytics-and-reporting) — plus the absence of \"did AI write this?\" comments. Never fabricated.\n"
